        <item>
            <title>0.8</title>
            <pubDate>Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:24:07 -0700</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>660</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.8</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.8 (build 660)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Optimized media keeps a movie&#8217;s own color.</b> Only EXR renders (and anything you have declared as the scene-linear working space) are encoded through the log curve. Movies and PNG, JPG, TIFF and DPX sequences &#8212; display-referred or camera log alike &#8212; now go straight through, so the stand-in is the original&#8217;s picture at a smaller size and decodes as whatever Input says. Correcting a clip&#8217;s input space later no longer makes its stand-in unusable. Stand-ins you already have keep working; delete and re-optimize one to move it to the new path.</li>
<li><b>Movies that are already light are left alone.</b> A movie under 50 Mbps &#8212; H.264, HEVC, ProRes Proxy review files &#8212; gains nothing from a stand-in, so Optimize skips it and says how many it skipped. The header shows a <b>LIGHT</b> pill where a stand-in would show OPTIMIZED, and the playlist shows a small hare in the lightning-bolt column. To optimize them anyway, turn off File &#9656; Optimized Media &#9656; Skip Movies That Are Already Light.</li>
<li><b>MXF is its own format.</b> MXF files are treated as camera footage: they default toward a camera log space rather than Rec.709, and the input space you choose for MXFs is remembered separately, so it never changes your mp4s and movs.</li>
<li>Fixed: stand-ins made with no color management came out too bright.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
